# Principals allowed to sign blamepost commits and tags.
#
# The format is the one `git` reads through `gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile` and
# `ssh-keygen -Y verify` reads through `-f`: a principal, the namespace the
# key may sign in, and the key itself. `namespaces="git"` confines each key
# to git objects, so a signature made for anything else does not verify here.
#
# Verify this file out of band before trusting it, because it lives in the
# repository whose tags it is used to check. Two independent anchors:
#
#   SECURITY.md records the SHA256 fingerprint of every key below, and its
#   own history is commit-signed, so an edit to either is visible.
#
#   GitHub publishes the maintainer's signing keys, and every key below
#   appears among them:
#     gh api users/dotCooCoo/ssh_signing_keys --jq '.[].key'
#   That list is a superset. It carries keys used for other projects, so
#   finding a key there is not on its own a reason to trust it for this one;
#   this file is what decides that. Note also that
#   https://github.com/dotCooCoo.keys is a DIFFERENT list again. It returns
#   authentication keys, and a key registered for signing alone does not
#   appear there.
#
# A key is added here in the same commit that records its fingerprint in
# SECURITY.md. A key is removed only when every tag it signed has been
# re-signed or is no longer offered, because removing it turns each of those
# signatures into a verification failure that reads like tampering.

security@blamejs.com namespaces="git" ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIF9jouzSOMrodVB6exBzW1f05wjbzLYW/85v4Rfh+bP3
